/* Microbiology 6–7: Virology */
Z("micro6",0,"m",["Viruses cannot multiply on their own; they hijack a host cell's machinery through steps: attachment, penetration, uncoating, synthesis of parts, assembly and release.","A virus is a pirated USB drive: plug it in (attach, enter), it unpacks (uncoat), forces the computer to copy it (synthesis), packages copies (assembly) and sends them out (release).",["Adsorption: viral attachment protein binds a specific receptor (HIV gp120–CD4, influenza HA–sialic acid).","Penetration: endocytosis, membrane fusion, or translocation; uncoating releases genome.","Biosynthesis: early proteins (enzymes) → genome replication → late proteins (structural); DNA viruses mostly in nucleus (except pox), RNA viruses in cytoplasm (except influenza, retro).","Maturation/assembly and release: lysis (naked viruses) or budding (enveloped viruses gain envelope). Eclipse phase = no infectious virus inside cell."],"Virus replication 'A-P-U-S-A-R': Attach, Penetrate, Uncoat, Synthesise, Assemble, Release."],
"Viruses are obligate intracellular parasites that replicate only within living cells by directing host machinery. The replication cycle (one-step growth curve) includes: (1) adsorption — specific binding of viral attachment proteins to host cell receptors (e.g., HIV gp120 to CD4 with CCR5/CXCR4, influenza haemagglutinin to sialic acid, poliovirus to CD155, rabies to acetylcholine receptor, EBV to CD21), determining host and tissue tropism; (2) penetration — receptor-mediated endocytosis (viropexis), fusion of envelope with plasma membrane, or translocation; (3) uncoating — release of viral nucleic acid from capsid (lysosomal enzymes, low pH); during the eclipse phase no infectious virus can be recovered; (4) biosynthesis — transcription of mRNA, translation of early proteins (enzymes such as polymerases), replication of the genome, and synthesis of late structural proteins; dsDNA viruses replicate in the nucleus using host or viral DNA polymerase (poxviruses in cytoplasm); positive-sense RNA viruses act directly as mRNA (picornaviruses) and replicate in cytoplasm; negative-sense RNA viruses carry RNA-dependent RNA polymerase; retroviruses use reverse transcriptase to make DNA that integrates into host genome; hepadnaviruses replicate via an RNA intermediate; (5) assembly/maturation — capsid proteins self-assemble around genomes; (6) release — naked viruses by cell lysis; enveloped viruses by budding, acquiring an envelope from host membranes carrying viral glycoproteins. Abortive and defective infections may occur. Understanding these steps is the basis of antiviral therapy (entry inhibitors, uncoating blockers like amantadine, polymerase inhibitors, protease and integrase inhibitors, neuraminidase inhibitors).",
[["Steps and examples",[["Step","Event","Example / drug target"],["Adsorption","Viral protein binds receptor","HIV–CD4; maraviroc blocks CCR5"],["Penetration","Endocytosis or fusion","Enfuvirtide blocks HIV fusion"],["Uncoating","Genome released","Amantadine (influenza M2)"],["Biosynthesis","mRNA, proteins, genome replication","Acyclovir, NRTIs, remdesivir"],["Assembly","Capsid + genome","Protease inhibitors (HIV)"],["Release","Lysis / budding","Oseltamivir (neuraminidase)"]]],
["Site of replication",["DNA viruses: nucleus (except poxviruses — cytoplasm)","RNA viruses: cytoplasm (except influenza and retroviruses — nuclear phase)","Retroviruses: reverse transcription → integration (provirus)"]]],
{c:"Each step of viral replication is a potential target for antiviral drugs; the receptor determines which cells a virus infects."});

Z("micro6",1,"m",["Viruses can be grown only in living cells: in animals, embryonated hen's eggs, or cell (tissue) cultures — the most widely used method today.","Viruses need a living 'host farm': a lab animal, a developing chick egg, or layers of cells growing in a bottle.",["Animal inoculation: suckling mice for coxsackie and arboviruses (historical, now limited).","Embryonated eggs (8–11 days): chorioallantoic membrane (pocks — pox, HSV), allantoic cavity (influenza, mumps), amniotic cavity (primary influenza isolation), yolk sac (Chlamydia, Rickettsia).","Cell culture types: primary (monkey kidney), diploid cell strains (WI-38, MRC-5), continuous cell lines (HeLa, HEp-2, Vero).","Detection of growth: cytopathic effect, haemadsorption, interference, immunofluorescence, PCR."],"Egg routes 'CAAY': CAM, Allantoic, Amniotic, Yolk sac."],
"Viruses, being obligate intracellular parasites, are cultivated in living systems. (1) Animal inoculation: the first method; suckling mice (intracerebral, intraperitoneal) for coxsackieviruses, arboviruses and rabies; used for pathogenesis studies and isolation where cell cultures fail; limited now by ethics and cost. (2) Embryonated hen's eggs (8–11 days old; Goodpasture, Burnet): chorioallantoic membrane — visible pocks (variola, vaccinia, HSV); allantoic cavity — influenza and mumps (vaccine production); amniotic cavity — primary isolation of influenza; yolk sac — Chlamydia, Rickettsia, some viruses. (3) Tissue (cell) culture — most widely used: organ culture (ciliated tracheal rings for coronaviruses), explant culture, and cell culture (monolayers). Types of cell culture: primary cell cultures (normal cells from tissues, limited to few divisions — monkey kidney, human amnion, chick embryo fibroblast); diploid cell strains (from fetal tissue, up to ~50 divisions, retain diploid karyotype — WI-38, MRC-5 human fetal lung; used for vaccines); continuous cell lines (from tumours or transformed cells, indefinite growth, aneuploid — HeLa, HEp-2, KB, Vero, BHK-21, McCoy). Detection of viral growth: cytopathic effect (rounding, syncytia — measles, RSV; inclusion bodies), haemadsorption (influenza, parainfluenza), interference (rubella with echovirus), metabolic inhibition, transformation, immunofluorescence or ELISA for viral antigens, electron microscopy, PCR. Shell vial technique (centrifugation-enhanced culture with early antigen detection) speeds diagnosis (CMV). Applications: diagnosis, vaccine production, research, antiviral testing.",
[["Methods",[["Method","Examples / uses"],["Animal inoculation","Suckling mice: coxsackie, arboviruses, rabies"],["Embryonated egg — CAM","Pox, HSV (pocks)"],["Embryonated egg — allantoic","Influenza, mumps, NDV (vaccine)"],["Embryonated egg — amniotic","Primary influenza isolation"],["Embryonated egg — yolk sac","Chlamydia, Rickettsia"],["Cell culture","Most viruses; diagnosis and vaccines"]]],
["Types of cell culture",[["Type","Features","Examples"],["Primary","Limited life, from tissue","Monkey kidney, human amnion"],["Diploid cell strain","~50 divisions, normal karyotype","WI-38, MRC-5 (vaccines)"],["Continuous cell line","Indefinite, aneuploid","HeLa, HEp-2, Vero, BHK-21"]]],
["Detection of growth",["Cytopathic effect (CPE): rounding, syncytia, inclusions","Haemadsorption","Interference","Immunofluorescence, ELISA","Electron microscopy, PCR"]]],
{c:"Cell culture has replaced most animal and egg inoculation for diagnosis, though eggs remain important for influenza vaccine production; molecular methods now complement culture."});

Z("micro6",2,"m",["Viral interference is when infection with one virus blocks infection by another; interactions between viruses also include recombination, complementation and phenotypic mixing.","Like a house already occupied by one tenant who prevents a second from moving in — partly through alarm signals called interferons.",["Interference: one virus inhibits another; basis — interferon production, receptor blockade, competition.","Used in OPV (gut interference by enteroviruses lowers efficacy), rubella detection (interference with echovirus).","Genetic interactions: recombination, reassortment (influenza — antigenic shift), multiplicity reactivation.","Non-genetic: complementation, phenotypic mixing, pseudotype formation (HDV coated with HBsAg)."],"Interference = one virus 'blocks' another (interferon)."],
"When two viruses infect the same host or cell, they may interact. Interference: infection with one virus inhibits replication of a second (homologous or heterologous) virus; mechanisms include production of interferons (induced by the first virus, protecting other cells), blockade or destruction of receptors (e.g., influenza neuraminidase destroying receptors), competition for replication machinery, and defective interfering particles. Applications: rubella virus detected in cell culture by its interference with echovirus CPE; OPV take is reduced by enterovirus infections in tropics (hence multiple doses and pulse polio); interference between OPV serotypes in trivalent vaccine. Genetic interactions: recombination (exchange of genetic segments between related viruses), reassortment in segmented viruses (influenza, rotavirus — basis of antigenic shift and new pandemic strains), multiplicity reactivation (inactivated viruses reconstitute infectious virus), marker rescue. Non-genetic interactions: complementation (one virus supplies a missing gene product to another, e.g., adeno-associated virus needs adenovirus), phenotypic mixing (genome of one virus in capsid of another), pseudotype formation and transcapsidation (hepatitis D virus uses HBsAg as envelope). Interferons are host proteins (type I α, β; type II γ) induced by viral double-stranded RNA; they act on neighbouring cells to induce antiviral proteins (protein kinase R, 2'-5' oligoadenylate synthetase, RNase L), are not virus-specific but are species-specific, and are used therapeutically (hepatitis B, C).",
[["Types of viral interactions",[["Interaction","Example"],["Interference","Rubella–echovirus; enteroviruses with OPV"],["Recombination","Polio vaccine-derived strains"],["Reassortment","Influenza antigenic shift"],["Complementation","AAV with adenovirus"],["Phenotypic mixing/pseudotype","HDV with HBsAg coat"]]],
["Interferons",[["Type","Source","Action"],["IFN-α","Leucocytes","Antiviral"],["IFN-β","Fibroblasts","Antiviral"],["IFN-γ","T cells, NK","Immunomodulatory, macrophage activation"]]]],
{c:"Viral interactions shape vaccine effectiveness and the emergence of new strains; interferon-mediated interference is a key innate antiviral defence."});

Z("micro6",3,"m",["Inclusion bodies are clumps of viral parts or cell debris seen inside infected cells under the microscope; their site (nucleus or cytoplasm) and type help identify the virus.","Like fingerprints left by a burglar inside a house: each virus leaves characteristic marks in the nucleus or cytoplasm.",["Intracytoplasmic: Negri bodies (rabies), Guarnieri (vaccinia), Paschen (variola), Bollinger (fowlpox), molluscum bodies, Henderson–Patterson.","Intranuclear: Cowdry type A (herpes, yellow fever — Torres), Cowdry type B (polio, adenovirus).","Both nuclear + cytoplasmic: measles (Warthin–Finkeldey giant cells), CMV ('owl's eye').","Negri bodies in hippocampus and Purkinje cells — diagnostic of rabies."],"Rabies = Negri; Pox = Guarnieri; Herpes = Cowdry A; CMV = Owl's eye."],
"Inclusion bodies are distinct structures seen in virus-infected cells by light microscopy after staining (Giemsa, H&E, Seller's). They may represent aggregates of virions, viral components or degenerative changes and are either acidophilic or basophilic, intracytoplasmic, intranuclear or both. Their demonstration aids diagnosis. Intracytoplasmic inclusions: Negri bodies (rabies; round/oval eosinophilic bodies with basophilic inner granules in neurons of hippocampus and Purkinje cells of cerebellum — Seller's stain), Guarnieri bodies (vaccinia, variola), Paschen bodies (variola, elementary bodies), Bollinger bodies (fowlpox), molluscum bodies (Henderson–Patterson, molluscum contagiosum), Halberstaedter–Prowazek bodies (Chlamydia trachomatis — bacterial). Intranuclear inclusions: Cowdry type A (acidophilic with halo; herpes simplex, varicella-zoster, yellow fever — Torres bodies), Cowdry type B (adenovirus, poliovirus), basophilic in adenovirus. Both intranuclear and intracytoplasmic: measles (multinucleate Warthin–Finkeldey giant cells with inclusions), cytomegalovirus ('owl's eye' intranuclear inclusion with smaller cytoplasmic inclusions in enlarged cells). Tzanck smear of vesicle base shows multinucleated giant cells with intranuclear inclusions (HSV, VZV).",
[["Classification",[["Location","Inclusion","Virus"],["Cytoplasmic","Negri bodies","Rabies"],["Cytoplasmic","Guarnieri bodies","Vaccinia, variola"],["Cytoplasmic","Paschen bodies","Variola"],["Cytoplasmic","Molluscum (Henderson–Patterson)","Molluscum contagiosum"],["Cytoplasmic","Bollinger bodies","Fowlpox"],["Nuclear","Cowdry type A","HSV, VZV, yellow fever (Torres)"],["Nuclear","Cowdry type B","Adenovirus, poliovirus"],["Both","Owl's eye","CMV"],["Both","Warthin–Finkeldey cells","Measles"]]]],
{c:"Characteristic inclusion bodies remain useful histological clues — Negri bodies for rabies, owl's eye inclusions for CMV — though molecular tests are now preferred."});

Z("micro6",4,"m",["Bacteriophages are viruses that infect bacteria; they have a head (containing DNA) and a tail for injecting it. They follow lytic (kill the bacterium) or lysogenic (hide in its DNA) cycles.","A phage is a tiny syringe that lands on a bacterium and injects its DNA; then it either takes over the factory immediately (lytic) or quietly joins the staff (lysogenic).",["Structure (T-even phage): icosahedral head with dsDNA, tail with sheath, base plate, tail fibres.","Lytic cycle: adsorption → injection → synthesis → assembly → lysis (virulent phages) — plaques on lawn.","Lysogenic cycle: temperate phage integrates as prophage; may confer new properties (lysogenic conversion — diphtheria, cholera, botulinum toxins).","Uses: phage typing (S. aureus, Salmonella), transduction, phage therapy, molecular biology."],"Phage cycles: Lytic = Lyse; Lysogenic = Lie low (prophage)."],
"Bacteriophages are viruses that infect bacteria (Twort 1915, d'Hérelle 1917). Morphology (T-even coliphage): a hexagonal (icosahedral) head of protein enclosing double-stranded DNA; a tail consisting of a hollow core surrounded by a contractile sheath, ending in a base plate with six tail fibres and spikes. Other phages may be filamentous or have RNA. Life cycle: lytic (virulent) cycle — adsorption of tail fibres to specific receptors on the bacterial wall; penetration by contraction of the sheath and injection of nucleic acid (capsid remains outside — 'ghost'); eclipse phase with synthesis of early proteins (shutting down host), phage DNA replication, late structural proteins; assembly of mature phages; release by lysis of the bacterium (by lysozyme/holins), releasing 50–200 progeny (burst size); visible as clear plaques on a bacterial lawn. Lysogenic (temperate) cycle — phage DNA integrates into the bacterial chromosome as a prophage and replicates with it; the bacterium is lysogenic; induction (UV, chemicals) can switch to lytic cycle. Lysogenic conversion confers new properties: toxin production (C. diphtheriae β-phage — diphtheria toxin; V. cholerae CTXφ — cholera toxin; C. botulinum; S. pyogenes — erythrogenic toxin; E. coli — Shiga-like toxin), changes in antigenicity (Salmonella O antigens). Applications: phage typing for epidemiological tracing (S. aureus, S. Typhi Vi-phage typing), transduction of genes, cloning vectors (λ), phage display, detection of bacteria, and phage therapy for resistant infections.",
[["Lytic vs lysogenic",[["Feature","Lytic","Lysogenic"],["Phage","Virulent","Temperate"],["Phage DNA","Replicates independently","Integrates as prophage"],["Host cell","Lysed","Survives, divides"],["Outcome","Progeny release, plaques","Lysogenic conversion, immunity to superinfection"]]],
["Applications",["Phage typing (S. aureus, Salmonella Typhi)","Transduction, genetic engineering vectors","Phage therapy for MDR infections","Phage display technology"]]],
{c:"Bacteriophages are important tools in microbiology and genetics; lysogenic conversion explains how some bacteria acquire powerful toxins."});

Z("micro6",5,"m",["Herpesviruses are large enveloped DNA viruses that cause lifelong latent infection with periodic reactivation; they include HSV-1, HSV-2, VZV, EBV, CMV and HHV-6, 7, 8.","Herpesviruses never truly leave: after the first infection they hide in nerves or blood cells and wake up when immunity dips.",["Classification: α (HSV-1, HSV-2, VZV — latent in neurons), β (CMV, HHV-6, 7 — latent in monocytes/glands), γ (EBV, HHV-8 — latent in B cells, oncogenic).","HSV-1: oral (gingivostomatitis, cold sores), encephalitis (temporal lobe); HSV-2: genital herpes, neonatal herpes.","VZV: chickenpox (primary), shingles (reactivation in dermatome).","Lab: Tzanck smear (multinucleated giant cells), PCR (CSF for encephalitis), viral culture, DFA, serology. Treatment: acyclovir."],"Herpes subfamilies: Alpha = nerves; Beta = CMV; Gamma = EBV (cancer)."],
"Herpesviridae are large (150–200 nm), enveloped viruses with icosahedral capsid, tegument and linear double-stranded DNA; they replicate in the nucleus, produce Cowdry type A intranuclear inclusions and multinucleated giant cells, and establish lifelong latency with reactivation. Classification: Alphaherpesvirinae — HSV-1, HSV-2, VZV (short replicative cycle, cytolytic, latent in sensory ganglia neurons); Betaherpesvirinae — CMV, HHV-6, HHV-7 (slow, cytomegalic, latent in monocytes, salivary glands, kidney); Gammaherpesvirinae — EBV, HHV-8 (latent in lymphocytes, oncogenic). HSV-1 spreads by saliva and causes acute gingivostomatitis, herpes labialis (cold sores), keratoconjunctivitis (dendritic ulcer), herpetic whitlow, encephalitis (temporal lobe; commonest sporadic viral encephalitis) and eczema herpeticum; latent in trigeminal ganglion. HSV-2 spreads sexually and causes genital herpes, neonatal herpes (from birth canal — disseminated, CNS, skin) and aseptic meningitis; latent in sacral ganglia. VZV causes varicella (chickenpox; airborne, vesicular rash in crops, centripetal) and herpes zoster (shingles; painful dermatomal vesicles; post-herpetic neuralgia); latent in dorsal root and cranial ganglia. Laboratory diagnosis: Tzanck smear of vesicle base (multinucleated giant cells — does not distinguish HSV from VZV); direct immunofluorescence; viral culture (rapid CPE in HEp-2, Vero); PCR (gold standard; CSF PCR for HSV encephalitis); serology (IgM for primary, IgG for past). Treatment: acyclovir, valacyclovir, famciclovir; foscarnet for resistant. Prevention: varicella vaccine (live), zoster vaccine.",
[["Classification",[["Subfamily","Viruses","Latency site"],["Alpha","HSV-1, HSV-2, VZV","Sensory ganglia"],["Beta","CMV, HHV-6, HHV-7","Monocytes, glands"],["Gamma","EBV, HHV-8","B lymphocytes"]]],
["Diseases",[["Virus","Primary","Reactivation"],["HSV-1","Gingivostomatitis, keratitis, encephalitis","Cold sores"],["HSV-2","Genital herpes, neonatal herpes","Recurrent genital lesions"],["VZV","Chickenpox","Shingles"],["CMV","Congenital CMV, mononucleosis-like","Retinitis, colitis in AIDS/transplant"],["EBV","Infectious mononucleosis","Lymphomas, NPC"],["HHV-6","Roseola infantum","—"],["HHV-8","—","Kaposi sarcoma"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["Tzanck smear: multinucleated giant cells, Cowdry A","DFA on vesicle scrapings","Culture: rapid CPE","PCR (CSF for HSV encephalitis)","Serology: IgM (primary), IgG"]]],
{c:"Herpesviruses share latency and reactivation; PCR has become the key diagnostic tool and acyclovir the mainstay of therapy."});

Z("micro6",6,"m",["CMV and EBV are herpesviruses: CMV causes congenital infection and serious disease in transplant and AIDS patients; EBV causes infectious mononucleosis and is linked to several cancers.","CMV swells infected cells into giant 'owl's eye' cells; EBV transforms B cells, sometimes into cancers.",["CMV: commonest congenital viral infection (deafness, microcephaly, periventricular calcification); CMV retinitis, colitis, pneumonitis in AIDS/transplant.","CMV lab: owl's eye inclusions, shell vial culture, pp65 antigenaemia, PCR (viral load); treatment ganciclovir.","EBV: infectious mononucleosis (atypical lymphocytes, heterophile antibodies); receptor CD21.","EBV cancers: Burkitt lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, Hodgkin lymphoma, post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder."],"CMV = Cytomegaly (owl's eye); EBV = B cells & cancers."],
"Cytomegalovirus (HHV-5), a betaherpesvirus, causes enlargement of infected cells with large intranuclear 'owl's eye' and cytoplasmic inclusions. Transmission: saliva, urine, breast milk, sexual contact, blood transfusion, organ transplant and transplacental. Most infections are asymptomatic; diseases include congenital CMV (commonest congenital infection — hepatosplenomegaly, jaundice, petechiae, microcephaly, periventricular calcification, chorioretinitis, sensorineural deafness), perinatal infection, heterophile-negative mononucleosis in adults, post-transfusion syndrome, and severe disease in immunocompromised hosts (AIDS — retinitis, colitis, oesophagitis, encephalitis at CD4 < 50; transplant — pneumonitis, hepatitis, graft dysfunction). Diagnosis: histology (owl's eye cells), shell vial culture with early antigen detection, pp65 antigenaemia, quantitative PCR (viral load), IgM serology/avidity (pregnancy), urine PCR in neonates. Treatment: ganciclovir, valganciclovir, foscarnet, cidofovir, letermovir (prophylaxis). Epstein–Barr virus (HHV-4), a gammaherpesvirus, infects B cells via CD21 and oropharyngeal epithelium; transmitted by saliva. Primary infection in childhood is often silent; in adolescents it causes infectious mononucleosis (fever, pharyngitis, lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, atypical lymphocytes — CD8 T cells, heterophile antibodies detected by Paul–Bunnell or Monospot). EBV-associated malignancies: endemic Burkitt lymphoma (with malaria cofactor), nasopharyngeal carcinoma (southern China), Hodgkin lymphoma (mixed cellularity), post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorder, primary CNS lymphoma in AIDS, NK/T-cell lymphoma, gastric carcinoma; also oral hairy leukoplakia. Diagnosis: heterophile antibodies, EBV-specific antibodies (VCA IgM — acute; EBNA — past), PCR (viral load in transplant), EBER in situ hybridisation in tumours.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","CMV","EBV"],["Subfamily","Beta","Gamma"],["Target cells","Epithelial, monocytes, many","B cells (CD21), epithelium"],["Hallmark","Owl's eye inclusions","Atypical lymphocytes"],["Congenital disease","Yes (commonest)","No"],["Mononucleosis","Heterophile-negative","Heterophile-positive"],["Malignancy","—","Burkitt, NPC, Hodgkin, PTLD"],["Immunocompromised","Retinitis, colitis, pneumonitis","PTLD, CNS lymphoma, hairy leukoplakia"],["Treatment","Ganciclovir","Supportive; rituximab for PTLD"]]]],
{c:"CMV and EBV are ubiquitous herpesviruses that are mild in normal hosts but serious in the fetus and the immunocompromised; EBV is also a major oncogenic virus."});

Z("micro6",7,"m",["Oncogenic viruses are viruses that can cause cancer, either by carrying cancer genes (oncogenes) or by switching off the cell's tumour-suppressor brakes.","Some viruses bring their own accelerator pedal (oncogene); others jam the cell's brakes (p53, Rb).",["DNA oncogenic viruses: HPV, HBV, EBV, HHV-8, Merkel cell polyomavirus; (animal: SV40, adenovirus, polyoma).","RNA oncogenic viruses: HTLV-1, HCV; (animal: Rous sarcoma virus — retroviruses with v-onc).","Mechanisms: integration and insertional mutagenesis, viral oncoproteins (HPV E6/E7, EBV LMP-1), chronic inflammation (HBV, HCV).","Prevention: HPV and HBV vaccines."],"Oncogenic viruses 'HHEHMH': HPV, HBV, EBV, HHV-8, Merkel, HTLV-1 + HCV."],
"Oncogenic (tumour) viruses can transform cells and cause tumours in animals or humans. DNA tumour viruses: papillomaviruses (HPV 16, 18), hepadnaviruses (HBV), herpesviruses (EBV, HHV-8), polyomaviruses (Merkel cell polyomavirus; SV40, BK, JC in animals), adenoviruses (tumours in rodents only), poxviruses (molluscum, Shope fibroma — benign). Their oncogenes are viral genes needed for replication that bind and inactivate tumour suppressor proteins (HPV E6 → p53 degradation; E7 → Rb; SV40 large T antigen → p53 and Rb; adenovirus E1A and E1B). RNA tumour viruses: retroviruses — acute transforming viruses carrying viral oncogenes (v-onc, e.g., v-src of Rous sarcoma virus, derived from cellular proto-oncogenes), slow transforming viruses causing insertional activation of cellular proto-oncogenes (avian leukosis virus), and HTLV-1 (Tax protein activating cellular genes; adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma); HCV (flavivirus) acts indirectly through chronic inflammation, regeneration and cirrhosis. Viral oncogenesis requires cofactors (immunosuppression, other carcinogens, genetic factors).",
[["Oncogenic viruses",[["Virus","Genome","Cancer"],["HPV 16, 18","DNA","Cervical, anal, oropharyngeal, penile carcinoma"],["HBV","DNA","Hepatocellular carcinoma"],["EBV","DNA","Burkitt, NPC, Hodgkin, PTLD"],["HHV-8","DNA","Kaposi sarcoma, primary effusion lymphoma"],["Merkel cell polyomavirus","DNA","Merkel cell carcinoma"],["HTLV-1","RNA (retro)","Adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma"],["HCV","RNA","Hepatocellular carcinoma, B-cell lymphoma"]]],
["Mechanisms",["Viral oncoproteins inactivate p53 and Rb (HPV, polyoma)","Insertional mutagenesis (retroviruses, HBV)","Viral oncogenes (v-onc) — transducing retroviruses","Transactivation of host genes (HTLV-1 Tax, HBx)","Chronic inflammation and regeneration (HBV, HCV)"]]],
{c:"About 15% of human cancers are virus-associated; vaccines against HPV and HBV are effective cancer-prevention tools."});

Z("micro6",8,"m",["Several viruses are proven causes of human cancers: HPV (cervical cancer), HBV and HCV (liver cancer), EBV (lymphomas, nasopharyngeal cancer), HHV-8 (Kaposi sarcoma), HTLV-1 (T-cell leukaemia) and Merkel cell polyomavirus.","Each virus targets a 'favourite' organ for cancer: HPV the cervix, hepatitis viruses the liver, EBV the B cells and nose-throat, HHV-8 the vessels.",["HPV 16/18: cervix, anus, oropharynx, penis, vulva — E6/E7.","HBV/HCV: hepatocellular carcinoma.","EBV: Burkitt lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, Hodgkin lymphoma, PTLD, gastric carcinoma.","HHV-8: Kaposi sarcoma, primary effusion lymphoma, multicentric Castleman; HTLV-1: ATLL; MCPyV: Merkel cell carcinoma."],"Viruses & cancer: 'HPV-cervix, HBV/HCV-liver, EBV-B cells & nose, HHV8-Kaposi, HTLV-T cells'."],
"About 12–15% of human cancers worldwide are attributable to viruses. Human papillomavirus (high-risk types 16, 18, 31, 33, 45) causes carcinoma of the cervix (virtually all cases), anus, vulva, vagina, penis and oropharynx; E6 degrades p53 and E7 inactivates Rb. Hepatitis B virus causes hepatocellular carcinoma through integration, HBx protein and chronic necroinflammation; hepatitis C virus causes HCC via cirrhosis and is associated with B-cell non-Hodgkin lymphoma. Epstein–Barr virus is associated with endemic Burkitt lymphoma (with malaria), nasopharyngeal carcinoma, Hodgkin lymphoma, post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorders, primary CNS lymphoma in AIDS, extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma and some gastric carcinomas. Human herpesvirus 8 (KSHV) causes Kaposi sarcoma, primary effusion lymphoma and multicentric Castleman disease. Human T-lymphotropic virus type 1 causes adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma (and tropical spastic paraparesis). Merkel cell polyomavirus causes most Merkel cell carcinomas of the skin. Prevention: HPV vaccination and screening, HBV vaccination, HCV treatment, safe blood, control of HIV (immune restoration).",
[["Viruses and associated cancers",[["Virus","Cancer","Prevention"],["HPV","Cervical, anogenital, oropharyngeal","HPV vaccine, Pap/HPV screening"],["HBV","Hepatocellular carcinoma","HBV vaccine, antivirals"],["HCV","HCC, B-cell lymphoma","DAA therapy, safe blood"],["EBV","Burkitt, NPC, Hodgkin, PTLD","—"],["HHV-8","Kaposi sarcoma, PEL","HIV control"],["HTLV-1","Adult T-cell leukaemia/lymphoma","Screening blood, breastfeeding advice"],["MCPyV","Merkel cell carcinoma","—"]]]],
{c:"Recognising viral causes of cancer has made some cancers preventable through vaccination and antiviral therapy."});

Z("micro7",0,"m",["Picornaviruses are small, non-enveloped, positive-sense RNA viruses ('pico' = small); they include enteroviruses (polio, coxsackie, echo), rhinoviruses and hepatitis A virus.","Tiny RNA viruses with tough coats that survive the gut (enteroviruses) or love the cool nose (rhinoviruses).",["Enteroviruses: polioviruses 1–3, coxsackie A and B, echoviruses, enteroviruses 68–71; acid-stable, faeco-oral.","Rhinoviruses: acid-labile, grow at 33 °C, common cold (> 100 types).","Hepatovirus: hepatitis A virus.","Others: parechovirus, aphthovirus (foot-and-mouth disease)."],"Picorna = 'PEECORNA': Polio, Enteric, Echo, Coxsackie, Rhino, hepatitis A."],
"Picornaviridae are small (25–30 nm), non-enveloped, icosahedral viruses with single-stranded, positive-sense RNA that acts as mRNA and is translated into a polyprotein cleaved by viral proteases; they replicate in the cytoplasm. Important genera: Enterovirus — polioviruses types 1, 2, 3; coxsackievirus group A (herpangina, hand-foot-mouth disease, acute haemorrhagic conjunctivitis — A24) and group B (pleurodynia/Bornholm disease, myocarditis, pericarditis, neonatal sepsis, aseptic meningitis, possibly type 1 diabetes); echoviruses (aseptic meningitis, rashes); numbered enteroviruses — EV-68 (respiratory, acute flaccid myelitis), EV-70 (acute haemorrhagic conjunctivitis), EV-71 (hand-foot-mouth disease, encephalitis); enteroviruses are acid-stable, resist bile, replicate in the gut and spread faeco-orally. Rhinovirus — > 100 serotypes, acid-labile, optimal growth at 33 °C, cause common cold. Hepatovirus — hepatitis A virus. Parechovirus — neonatal sepsis and meningitis. Aphthovirus — foot-and-mouth disease of cattle. Cardiovirus — encephalomyocarditis virus.",
[["Classification",[["Genus","Members","Diseases"],["Enterovirus","Polio 1–3","Poliomyelitis"],["","Coxsackie A","Herpangina, HFMD, conjunctivitis"],["","Coxsackie B","Myocarditis, pleurodynia, meningitis"],["","Echovirus","Aseptic meningitis, rash"],["","EV 68–71","Respiratory illness, AHC, HFMD, encephalitis"],["Rhinovirus","> 100 types","Common cold"],["Hepatovirus","HAV","Hepatitis A"],["Parechovirus","HPeV","Neonatal sepsis"]]],
["Enterovirus vs rhinovirus",[["Feature","Enterovirus","Rhinovirus"],["Acid stability","Stable","Labile"],["Optimal temperature","37 °C","33 °C"],["Site","Gut","Nose"],["Transmission","Faeco-oral","Respiratory, fomites"]]]],
{c:"Picornaviruses are simple RNA viruses causing diseases from the common cold to paralytic polio; their acid stability determines whether they infect the gut."});

Z("micro7",1,"m",["Poliovirus is an enterovirus spread by faeco-oral route; in a small minority it invades the spinal cord's motor neurons, causing flaccid paralysis. Vaccination (OPV, IPV) prevents it.","Most infections are silent gut visitors; occasionally the virus slips into the bloodstream and attacks the motor cells of the spinal cord — the 'switches' for muscles.",["Three serotypes; type 1 commonest cause of paralysis; India polio-free since 2014.","Pathogenesis: ingestion → tonsils and Peyer's patches → viraemia → CNS (anterior horn cells).","Outcomes: inapparent (90–95%), abortive (minor illness), non-paralytic (aseptic meningitis), paralytic (< 1%) — asymmetric flaccid paralysis.","Lab: stool virus isolation (2 samples 24 h apart within 14 days of AFP — AFP surveillance), RT-PCR, intratypic differentiation (wild vs vaccine), serology."],"Polio: 'Anterior horn, Asymmetric, Acute flaccid, no sensory loss'."],
"Poliovirus is an enterovirus (types 1, 2, 3; type 1 most epidemic and paralytogenic; wild types 2 and 3 eradicated). Transmission is faeco-oral (and pharyngeal droplets early). Pathogenesis: virus multiplies in oropharynx and intestinal mucosa (tonsils, Peyer's patches), spreads to regional lymph nodes, causing minor viraemia, then multiplies in reticuloendothelial tissues, causing major viraemia; it may invade the CNS via blood–brain barrier or along nerves (retrograde axonal transport), selectively destroying motor neurons of the anterior horn of the spinal cord and brainstem (CD155 receptor). Provoking factors: intramuscular injections (provocation poliomyelitis), tonsillectomy, exertion, pregnancy. Clinical outcomes: inapparent infection (90–95%), abortive poliomyelitis (minor illness — fever, sore throat; 4–8%), non-paralytic poliomyelitis (aseptic meningitis; 1–2%), paralytic poliomyelitis (< 1%; spinal — acute asymmetrical flaccid paralysis, proximal > distal, lower limbs, no sensory loss; bulbar — cranial nerves, respiratory paralysis; bulbospinal); post-polio syndrome years later. Laboratory diagnosis: virus isolation from stool (two specimens 24–48 hours apart within 14 days of onset of paralysis, transported in cold chain), throat swab, CSF (rarely positive); cell culture (RD, L20B cells), identification by neutralisation, intratypic differentiation by RT-PCR (wild vs vaccine-derived); serology (rising neutralising antibodies). Surveillance: acute flaccid paralysis (AFP) surveillance in children < 15 years with stool testing. Prophylaxis: OPV (Sabin, live attenuated; bivalent bOPV 1 and 3) — mucosal and herd immunity, risk of VAPP and cVDPV; IPV (Salk, killed; fractional intradermal IPV in UIP) — safe, systemic immunity. India was declared polio-free in 2014 (last case 2011).",
[["Pathogenesis",["Ingestion → oropharynx & gut lymphoid tissue","Minor viraemia → RE system → major viraemia","CNS invasion → anterior horn cell destruction","Asymmetric flaccid paralysis; no sensory loss"]],
["Clinical outcomes",[["Form","Frequency","Features"],["Inapparent","90–95%","No symptoms, immunity"],["Abortive","4–8%","Minor febrile illness"],["Non-paralytic","1–2%","Aseptic meningitis"],["Paralytic","< 1%","Flaccid paralysis (spinal, bulbar)"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["2 stool samples 24–48 h apart within 14 days of AFP onset","Culture on RD and L20B cells","Neutralisation for typing; ITD by RT-PCR","Serology: 4-fold rise in neutralising antibodies"]]],
{c:"Poliomyelitis is preventable by vaccination; AFP surveillance with stool testing is essential to sustain eradication."});

Z("micro7",2,"m",["OPV (oral, live attenuated Sabin vaccine) and IPV (injected, killed Salk vaccine) both prevent polio, but OPV also gives gut immunity and stops transmission, while IPV is safer.","OPV trains the gut guards at the entry gate and even immunises contacts; IPV trains the blood army only, but carries no risk of the vaccine virus misbehaving.",["OPV: oral drops, cheap, no needles, intestinal IgA (blocks transmission), herd immunity, useful in outbreaks.","OPV risks: VAPP, circulating vaccine-derived poliovirus (cVDPV), interference by gut enteroviruses, needs cold chain, contraindicated in immunodeficiency.","IPV: injection, safe in immunodeficiency, no VAPP, good serum antibody; little gut immunity.","India UIP: bOPV + fractional IPV (6 and 14 weeks)."],"OPV = Oral, Of gut immunity, Outbreak control; IPV = Injection, Immunocompromised safe."],
"Two types of polio vaccine are used. Oral polio vaccine (Sabin) contains live attenuated polioviruses (now bivalent — types 1 and 3; trivalent withdrawn in 2016 after type 2 eradication), given orally. It induces both intestinal (secretory IgA) and humoral immunity, prevents infection and transmission of wild virus, spreads to contacts giving herd immunity, is cheap, easy to administer (no needles; mass campaigns — pulse polio), and is effective in outbreak control. Disadvantages: rare vaccine-associated paralytic poliomyelitis (VAPP; about 1 per 2.7 million doses), emergence of circulating vaccine-derived polioviruses (cVDPV) in under-immunised populations, reduced efficacy in tropics due to interference by other enteroviruses and diarrhoea, heat-labile (needs strict cold chain; vaccine vial monitor), contraindicated in immunodeficient persons and their contacts. Inactivated polio vaccine (Salk) contains formalin-killed polioviruses of all three types, given by injection (IM or fractional intradermal 0.1 mL). It induces serum antibodies protecting against paralysis, is safe in immunodeficiency and pregnancy, carries no risk of VAPP or VDPV, is more heat-stable and can be combined with other vaccines; but it produces poor intestinal immunity (does not prevent gut infection and transmission), is costlier and needs trained personnel. Current Indian strategy: bOPV at birth, 6, 10, 14 weeks and booster, plus fractional IPV at 6 and 14 weeks (and 9 months in new schedule) to provide type 2 protection and boost immunity.",
[["OPV vs IPV",[["Feature","OPV (Sabin)","IPV (Salk)"],["Nature","Live attenuated","Formalin-killed"],["Route","Oral","IM / intradermal"],["Intestinal immunity","Yes (IgA)","Poor"],["Humoral immunity","Yes","Yes (good)"],["Prevents transmission","Yes","Little"],["Herd immunity","Yes (spread to contacts)","No"],["VAPP / cVDPV risk","Yes (rare)","No"],["Use in immunodeficiency","Contraindicated","Safe"],["Interference by enteroviruses","Yes","No"],["Cost","Low","Higher"],["Heat stability","Poor","Better"],["Outbreak control","Preferred","Less useful"],["Booster need","Multiple doses","Fewer"]]]],
{c:"OPV and IPV are complementary; combined schedules give gut and systemic immunity while reducing the risks of vaccine-derived polio."});

Z("micro7",3,"m",["Influenza virus is an enveloped, segmented RNA virus with surface spikes haemagglutinin (H) and neuraminidase (N); it causes seasonal flu epidemics and occasional pandemics.","The virus wears two types of spikes: H to grab onto cells, N to cut itself free; changing these spikes lets it escape our immune memory.",["Orthomyxovirus; types A (animals + humans, pandemics), B (humans), C (mild).","8 segments of negative-sense RNA; HA (binds sialic acid, haemagglutination) and NA (releases virions).","Pathogenesis: droplets → infects respiratory epithelium → necrosis, desquamation → secondary bacterial pneumonia (S. aureus, pneumococcus).","Lab: nasopharyngeal swab RT-PCR (preferred), rapid antigen tests, culture in eggs/MDCK cells (haemadsorption), HAI serology. Treatment: oseltamivir."],"Influenza 'H grabs, N cuts'; 8 segments → shift."],
"Influenza viruses (family Orthomyxoviridae) are pleomorphic, spherical (80–120 nm) or filamentous, enveloped viruses with helical nucleocapsid containing eight segments (types A and B) of negative-sense single-stranded RNA with RNA polymerase. The envelope bears two glycoprotein spikes: haemagglutinin (HA; trimer; binds sialic acid receptors on respiratory epithelium, agglutinates RBCs, mediates fusion; main neutralising antigen) and neuraminidase (NA; tetramer; cleaves sialic acid to release virions and prevent aggregation; target of oseltamivir); M2 ion channel protein (target of amantadine) and M1 matrix protein. Types (by NP and M antigens): A (humans, birds, pigs, horses; subtypes by HA H1–H18 and NA N1–N11; causes epidemics and pandemics), B (humans; epidemics), C (mild), D (cattle). Antigenic variation: drift (point mutations in HA/NA — seasonal epidemics) and shift (reassortment of gene segments — new subtypes, pandemics). Pathogenesis: droplets and fomites; virus binds ciliated epithelium of the respiratory tract, replicates, causes cell necrosis, loss of cilia and inflammation; viraemia rare; incubation 1–3 days. Clinical: sudden fever, chills, myalgia, headache, dry cough, sore throat; complications — primary viral pneumonia, secondary bacterial pneumonia (S. aureus, pneumococcus, H. influenzae), myositis, myocarditis, Reye syndrome (aspirin in children with influenza B); high-risk — elderly, infants, pregnant, chronic diseases. Laboratory diagnosis: nasopharyngeal/throat swab in viral transport medium; RT-PCR (rapid, typing and subtyping — gold standard), rapid antigen detection, immunofluorescence; virus isolation in embryonated eggs (amniotic/allantoic) or MDCK cells detected by haemagglutination/haemadsorption; serology — haemagglutination inhibition (4-fold rise). Treatment: oseltamivir, zanamivir, baloxavir. Prevention: annual inactivated (or live attenuated) vaccine updated by WHO each season.",
[["Structure",["Envelope with HA and NA spikes; M2 ion channel","Helical nucleocapsid; 8 RNA segments (negative sense)","RNA-dependent RNA polymerase (PB1, PB2, PA)"]],
["Types",[["Feature","A","B","C"],["Hosts","Humans, birds, pigs, horses","Humans","Humans"],["Segments","8","8","7"],["Antigenic shift","Yes","No","No"],["Disease","Epidemics, pandemics","Epidemics","Mild"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",["Nasopharyngeal swab in VTM","RT-PCR (typing, subtyping)","Rapid antigen tests, IF","Culture: eggs, MDCK cells — haemagglutination","Serology: HAI 4-fold rise"]]],
{c:"Influenza's segmented genome and surface antigens underlie its constant variation; RT-PCR diagnosis, neuraminidase inhibitors and annually updated vaccines are the mainstays of control."});

Z("micro7",4,"m",["Orthomyxoviruses (influenza) and paramyxoviruses (measles, mumps, RSV, parainfluenza) are both enveloped negative-sense RNA respiratory viruses but differ in genome, size and spikes.","Orthomyxo: small, segmented, separate H and N spikes. Paramyxo: larger, one long RNA, combined HN spike plus a fusion (F) protein that makes giant cells.",["Ortho: segmented genome (8), smaller (80–120 nm), replicates partly in nucleus, separate HA and NA, antigenic shift common.","Para: non-segmented, larger (150–300 nm), cytoplasmic replication, HN together (or H, or G), F protein → syncytia.","Para members: measles, mumps, parainfluenza, RSV, metapneumovirus, Nipah, Hendra.","Para: antigenically stable — one vaccine gives lasting immunity (measles, mumps)."],"Ortho = Original 8 segments, Shifting; Para = one Piece, Fusion protein, stable."],
"Orthomyxoviruses (influenza viruses A, B, C) and paramyxoviruses (parainfluenza, mumps, measles, RSV, human metapneumovirus, Nipah and Hendra viruses) are both enveloped, helical, negative-sense ssRNA viruses that infect the respiratory tract, but differ in several respects. Orthomyxoviruses are smaller (80–120 nm), have a segmented genome (7–8 segments), replicate partly in the nucleus (cap snatching), carry separate HA and NA spikes, lack a separate fusion protein (HA mediates fusion), show frequent antigenic variation (drift and shift) because of segmentation, and cause epidemics and pandemics. Paramyxoviruses are larger (150–300 nm), have a single non-segmented RNA molecule, replicate entirely in the cytoplasm, have haemagglutinin and neuraminidase on the same spike (HN — parainfluenza, mumps) or haemagglutinin only (H — measles) or neither (G protein — RSV), and possess a fusion (F) protein causing cell fusion and multinucleated giant cells (syncytia); they are antigenically stable, so infection or vaccination gives long-lasting immunity, and genetic reassortment does not occur. Haemolysis is a feature of paramyxoviruses.",
[["Differences",[["Feature","Orthomyxovirus","Paramyxovirus"],["Examples","Influenza A, B, C","Measles, mumps, parainfluenza, RSV, Nipah"],["Size","80–120 nm","150–300 nm"],["Genome","Segmented (7–8)","Non-segmented"],["Site of replication","Nucleus + cytoplasm","Cytoplasm"],["Spikes","Separate HA and NA","HN together, or H, or G"],["Fusion protein","Absent (HA does fusion)","F protein present"],["Syncytia","No","Yes (giant cells)"],["Haemolysis","No","Yes"],["Antigenic variation","Frequent (drift, shift)","Stable"],["Reassortment","Yes","No"],["Lasting immunity after infection","No (variation)","Yes"],["Vaccine","Annual updates","Single long-lasting (MMR)"]]]],
{c:"Genome segmentation explains influenza's antigenic instability, whereas the stable paramyxoviruses are controlled with durable vaccines like MMR."});

Z("micro7",5,"m",["Antigenic drift is a gradual small change in influenza's surface proteins causing seasonal epidemics; antigenic shift is a sudden major change producing a new subtype that can cause a pandemic.","Drift is like changing your hairstyle — friends still roughly recognise you; shift is like getting a new face — nobody recognises you, so everyone is susceptible.",["Drift: point mutations in HA/NA genes; occurs in influenza A and B; minor variants; epidemics every 2–3 years; reason for annual vaccine update.","Shift: genetic reassortment between human and animal (avian, swine) influenza A strains, or direct jump; new HA (± NA) subtype; pandemics.","Pigs as 'mixing vessels' (receptors for both human and avian viruses).","Pandemics: 1918 H1N1, 1957 H2N2, 1968 H3N2, 2009 H1N1."],"Drift = Drip-drip small changes; Shift = Sudden switch (pandemic)."],
"Antigenic variation of influenza viruses involves their surface glycoproteins haemagglutinin and neuraminidase. Antigenic drift is a gradual, continuous accumulation of point mutations in HA and NA genes (due to error-prone RNA polymerase lacking proofreading), selected by population immunity; it produces new strains related to previous ones; occurs in influenza A and B; partial immunity persists in the population; causes seasonal epidemics every year or few years; necessitates annual updating of vaccine composition. Antigenic shift is an abrupt, major change resulting in a new HA (with or without new NA) subtype to which the population has little or no immunity; it occurs only in influenza A, through genetic reassortment of RNA segments when a host cell (often pigs, the 'mixing vessel' with both α-2,3 and α-2,6 sialic acid receptors) is co-infected with human and animal (avian or swine) viruses, or by direct transmission of an animal virus to humans with adaptation; it causes pandemics with high attack rates. Examples: 1918 Spanish flu (H1N1), 1957 Asian flu (H2N2), 1968 Hong Kong flu (H3N2), 2009 swine flu (H1N1 pdm09). Avian H5N1 and H7N9 are pandemic threats.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Antigenic drift","Antigenic shift"],["Mechanism","Point mutations","Genetic reassortment / interspecies jump"],["Change","Minor, gradual","Major, abrupt"],["Types affected","A and B","A only"],["Subtype","Same","New HA ± NA"],["Population immunity","Partial","None"],["Result","Epidemics","Pandemics"],["Frequency","Every year","Every 10–40 years (unpredictable)"],["Example","Seasonal H3N2 variants","H1N1 2009, H2N2 1957"]]]],
{f:"Drift: point mutations in HA/NA → slightly new strain → epidemic\nShift: human + avian/swine viruses co-infect pig → reassortment\n → new HA/NA subtype → no immunity → pandemic",c:"Drift explains yearly flu epidemics and vaccine updates; shift explains unpredictable pandemics, which demand global surveillance."});

Z("micro7",6,"m",["Arboviruses are viruses spread by blood-sucking arthropods like mosquitoes and ticks; important ones in India are Japanese encephalitis, dengue and (globally) yellow fever.","Mosquitoes act as flying syringes, carrying viruses from animals (pigs, birds, monkeys) or other humans to us.",["Japanese encephalitis: flavivirus; Culex tritaeniorhynchus; pigs amplifiers, ardeid birds reservoir; encephalitis in children; JE vaccine in UIP (endemic areas).","Dengue: flavivirus, 4 serotypes; Aedes aegypti (day-biting); fever, rash, myalgia; severe dengue (DHF/DSS) on secondary infection with another serotype (antibody-dependent enhancement).","Yellow fever: flavivirus; Aedes; jungle and urban cycles; hepatitis, jaundice, haemorrhage, Councilman bodies; not in India; 17D vaccine mandatory for travel.","Lab: NS1 antigen (dengue days 1–5), IgM MAC-ELISA, RT-PCR, virus isolation; thrombocytopenia, haemoconcentration in dengue."],"Dengue = Aedes, Day biter, ADE in 2nd infection; JE = Culex, pigs; YF = Aedes, liver, Councilman."],
"Arboviruses (arthropod-borne viruses) multiply in blood-feeding arthropods and are transmitted to vertebrates by bite; they belong to several families (Flaviviridae, Togaviridae, Bunyavirales, Reoviridae). Japanese encephalitis virus (flavivirus) is maintained in a cycle among Culex mosquitoes (C. tritaeniorhynchus, C. vishnui), ardeid birds (herons, egrets — reservoirs) and pigs (amplifying hosts); humans are dead-end hosts. Most infections are silent; < 1% develop encephalitis (fever, headache, altered sensorium, seizures, movement disorders) with high mortality and neurological sequelae; children in rural rice-growing areas of Uttar Pradesh, Bihar, Assam are affected. Diagnosis: IgM capture ELISA in CSF and serum, RT-PCR. Prevention: live attenuated SA 14-14-2 or inactivated vaccine (UIP in endemic districts at 9 and 16–24 months), vector control, piggery relocation. Dengue virus (flavivirus; 4 serotypes DENV 1–4) is transmitted by Aedes aegypti (day-biting, breeds in clean stored water) and A. albopictus; urban human–mosquito cycle. Clinical: dengue fever (high fever, retro-orbital pain, myalgia — 'breakbone fever', rash, leucopenia, thrombocytopenia); severe dengue (dengue haemorrhagic fever and dengue shock syndrome — plasma leakage, haemoconcentration, bleeding, shock), more common on secondary infection with a different serotype due to antibody-dependent enhancement. Diagnosis: NS1 antigen (first 5 days), IgM MAC-ELISA (after day 5), RT-PCR, IgG for secondary infection; CBC — falling platelets, rising haematocrit; tourniquet test. Management: fluids, no NSAIDs; vaccine (Dengvaxia, TAK-003). Yellow fever virus (flavivirus) has jungle (sylvatic) cycle among monkeys and forest mosquitoes (Haemagogus, Aedes africanus) and urban cycle with Aedes aegypti; endemic in Africa and South America, not in India (though vector present). Clinical: fever, then toxic phase with hepatitis (jaundice — 'yellow'), haemorrhage (black vomit), renal failure; liver shows midzonal necrosis and Councilman bodies, Torres intranuclear inclusions. Prevention: live 17D vaccine (single dose, valid for life), mandatory international certificate for travellers from endemic areas; quarantine of unvaccinated travellers in India.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Japanese encephalitis","Dengue","Yellow fever"],["Family","Flavivirus","Flavivirus","Flavivirus"],["Vector","Culex tritaeniorhynchus","Aedes aegypti","Aedes aegypti, Haemagogus"],["Reservoir/amplifier","Ardeid birds, pigs","Humans","Monkeys"],["Disease","Encephalitis","Fever, DHF/DSS","Hepatitis, haemorrhage"],["India","Endemic","Endemic","Absent"],["Diagnosis","IgM ELISA CSF/serum","NS1, IgM, RT-PCR","IgM, RT-PCR, histology"],["Vaccine","SA 14-14-2 (UIP)","Limited","17D live"]]],
["Dengue: severe disease mechanism",["Secondary infection with heterologous serotype","Non-neutralising antibodies enhance entry into monocytes (ADE)","Cytokine storm → plasma leakage, haemoconcentration, shock","NS1-induced endothelial damage, thrombocytopenia"]]],
{i:"Arboviral infections are rising with urbanisation, climate change and vector expansion; dengue and JE are major public health problems in India.",f:"Vertebrate reservoir (birds/pigs/monkeys/humans) → mosquito (blood meal) → virus multiplies in mosquito (extrinsic incubation) → bite → human\nJE: Culex + pigs → encephalitis\nDengue: Aedes + humans → fever, DHF/DSS\nYF: Aedes/Haemagogus + monkeys → hepatitis",c:"Arboviral diseases are best controlled by vector control and vaccination; NS1 and IgM ELISA are key diagnostic tests in India."});

Z("micro7",7,"m",["Rabies virus is a bullet-shaped RNA virus spread by animal bites (mainly dogs in India) that travels along nerves to the brain and causes fatal encephalitis; it is 100% preventable by prompt wound care and vaccination.","The virus crawls slowly up nerves from the bite like a train on rails to reach the brain; post-exposure vaccine and immunoglobulin race to stop it before it arrives.",["Rhabdovirus, bullet shape, negative-sense RNA, envelope with G protein spikes (induces neutralising antibodies).","Pathogenesis: bite → multiplies in muscle → peripheral nerves (retrograde axonal transport) → CNS → centrifugal spread to salivary glands, cornea, skin.","Clinical: prodrome (paraesthesia at bite), furious rabies (hydrophobia, aerophobia, agitation) or paralytic; coma and death.","Lab: antemortem — corneal impression smear/skin biopsy (nuchal) DFA, saliva RT-PCR, serum/CSF antibodies; postmortem — Negri bodies, DFA on brain. PEP: wound wash, ARV (IM Essen/Zagreb or ID), RIG for category III."],"Rabies PEP: 'Wash (15 min soap), Vaccinate, Immunoglobulin (cat III)'."],
"Rabies virus (genus Lyssavirus, family Rhabdoviridae) is a bullet-shaped (180 × 75 nm) enveloped virus with helical nucleocapsid and single-stranded negative-sense RNA; envelope glycoprotein G spikes mediate attachment (acetylcholine receptor, NCAM, p75 neurotrophin receptor) and induce neutralising antibodies (basis of vaccines); nucleoprotein N is group-specific. Street virus (wild; variable incubation, Negri bodies) and fixed virus (passaged intracerebrally in rabbits by Pasteur; fixed short incubation, no Negri bodies, used for vaccines). Transmission: bites or scratches of rabid animals (dogs > 95% in India; cats, monkeys, jackals, bats, mongoose), licks on broken skin/mucosa, rarely aerosols, corneal and organ transplants. Pathogenesis: virus replicates in muscle at the bite site, enters peripheral nerves at neuromuscular junctions, travels by retrograde axonal transport (3–12 mm/day) to the spinal cord and brain (incubation 1–3 months; shorter with bites on face, head, hands and severe bites), causes encephalomyelitis (limbic system, brainstem) with minimal neuronal destruction, then spreads centrifugally along nerves to salivary glands (shedding in saliva), skin, cornea and other organs. Clinical: prodrome (fever, malaise, pain/paraesthesia at bite site), acute neurological phase — furious (encephalitic) form with hydrophobia (painful pharyngeal spasms on attempting to drink or seeing water), aerophobia, hyperexcitability, autonomic dysfunction; or paralytic (dumb) form with ascending flaccid paralysis; followed by coma and death (nearly 100% fatal). Laboratory diagnosis: antemortem — direct immunofluorescence of corneal impression smears and nuchal skin biopsy (hair follicle nerves), RT-PCR on saliva, CSF, skin; neutralising antibodies in serum/CSF (unvaccinated); virus isolation in mouse or neuroblastoma cells; postmortem — Negri bodies in hippocampus and cerebellar Purkinje cells (Seller's stain), DFA on brain tissue (gold standard). Prophylaxis: WHO exposure categories — I (touching, licks on intact skin — no PEP), II (nibbling, minor scratches without bleeding — wound management + vaccine), III (transdermal bites/scratches, licks on broken skin or mucosa, bat exposure — wound management + vaccine + rabies immunoglobulin). Wound care: immediate washing with soap and running water for 15 minutes, antiseptic (povidone iodine), avoid suturing. Vaccines: cell culture vaccines (PCEC, HDCV, PVRV, PDEV) — IM Essen schedule (days 0, 3, 7, 14, 28) or 4-dose Zagreb; intradermal updated Thai Red Cross (2-site on days 0, 3, 7, 28). Rabies immunoglobulin (HRIG 20 IU/kg, ERIG 40 IU/kg, or monoclonal) infiltrated into and around wounds. Pre-exposure prophylaxis for high-risk groups (days 0, 7, 21/28). Control: dog vaccination and population control.",
[["Pathogenesis",["Bite → replication in muscle","Entry into peripheral nerves → retrograde axonal transport","Spinal cord → brain (encephalitis; Negri bodies)","Centrifugal spread → salivary glands, cornea, skin"]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",[["Stage","Specimen","Test"],["Antemortem","Corneal impression, nuchal skin biopsy","DFA"],["Antemortem","Saliva, CSF, skin","RT-PCR"],["Antemortem","Serum, CSF","Neutralising antibodies (RFFIT)"],["Postmortem","Brain (hippocampus, cerebellum)","Negri bodies, DFA (gold standard), RT-PCR"]]],
["Post-exposure prophylaxis",[["Category","Exposure","Action"],["I","Touch, lick on intact skin","No PEP"],["II","Nibbling, minor scratches without bleeding","Wound care + vaccine"],["III","Bites, scratches with bleeding, licks on broken skin/mucosa, bats","Wound care + vaccine + RIG"]]]],
{i:"Rabies is almost invariably fatal once symptoms appear, yet entirely preventable; India accounts for about a third of global rabies deaths.",x:[["Vaccine schedules",["IM Essen: days 0, 3, 7, 14, 28","IM Zagreb: 2 doses day 0, 1 each day 7, 21","ID (updated Thai Red Cross): 0.1 mL at 2 sites on days 0, 3, 7, 28","Previously vaccinated: 2 doses days 0, 3, no RIG"]]],f:"Bite → muscle replication → peripheral nerve (retrograde)\n → CNS encephalitis (Negri bodies) → salivary glands (saliva)\nPEP: wash 15 min → vaccine (0,3,7,14,28) + RIG for category III",c:"Rabies is diagnosed by DFA and RT-PCR on corneal, skin, saliva or brain specimens; prompt wound care, vaccination and immunoglobulin prevent virtually all deaths."});

Z("micro7",8,"m",["Hepatitis B virus is a small DNA virus spread by blood, sex and from mother to baby; it can cause acute hepatitis, chronic infection, cirrhosis and liver cancer, and is preventable by vaccine.","HBV is a stealthy virus that hides its DNA in liver cells; the immune attack against it, not the virus itself, damages the liver.",["Hepadnavirus; partially double-stranded circular DNA; replicates via RNA intermediate (reverse transcriptase).","Dane particle (42 nm, complete virion), plus spherical and tubular HBsAg particles (22 nm).","Transmission: blood, sexual, perinatal (vertical — 90% chronicity in newborns), needles.","Lab: HBsAg, HBeAg, anti-HBc IgM, anti-HBs, HBV DNA; prevention: recombinant HBsAg vaccine (birth dose), HBIG for newborns of HBsAg+ mothers and after needle-stick."],"HBV chronicity: 90% newborns, 30% children, 5% adults."],
"Hepatitis B virus (Hepadnaviridae) is a 42 nm enveloped virus (Dane particle) with an outer envelope containing surface antigen (HBsAg) and an inner icosahedral core (HBcAg) enclosing partially double-stranded circular DNA, DNA polymerase with reverse transcriptase activity, and HBeAg (secreted form of core protein). Infected serum also contains excess non-infectious 22 nm spherical and tubular HBsAg particles. Genome has four overlapping genes: S (surface), C (core and e), P (polymerase), X (HBx transactivator). Replication: entry via NTCP receptor; cccDNA in nucleus; transcription to pregenomic RNA; reverse transcription to DNA within core particles. HBsAg subtypes (adw, ayw etc.) and genotypes A–J. Transmission: parenteral (blood transfusion, unsafe injections, needle-stick, IV drug use, tattooing, dialysis), sexual, perinatal (vertical — mainly at birth from HBeAg-positive mothers), horizontal in households; highly infectious and stable. Pathogenesis: the virus is not directly cytopathic; liver injury results from cytotoxic T-cell attack on infected hepatocytes. Outcomes depend on age: acute hepatitis (incubation 30–180 days; prodrome with serum sickness-like features, jaundice) with recovery in > 95% of adults; fulminant hepatitis (< 1%); chronic infection (HBsAg > 6 months) in 90% of infected neonates, 25–50% of children, 5% of adults — inactive carrier, chronic hepatitis, cirrhosis and hepatocellular carcinoma; extrahepatic — polyarteritis nodosa, membranous glomerulonephritis; HDV co-infection or superinfection. Laboratory diagnosis: LFTs; serological markers — HBsAg (first marker; chronic if > 6 months), HBeAg (replication, infectivity), IgM anti-HBc (acute; window period), IgG anti-HBc (past/chronic), anti-HBe, anti-HBs (immunity — recovery or vaccination); HBV DNA by quantitative PCR (viral load); liver biopsy/elastography. Prophylaxis: recombinant HBsAg vaccine (birth dose within 24 h, then with pentavalent at 6, 10, 14 weeks; adults 0, 1, 6 months; anti-HBs ≥ 10 mIU/mL protective); hepatitis B immunoglobulin for babies of HBsAg-positive mothers (with vaccine within 12 h), after needle-stick injury in non-immune, sexual contacts; screening of blood donors; universal precautions; antivirals (tenofovir) in pregnancy with high viral load.",
[["Structure",["Dane particle: 42 nm, envelope (HBsAg), core (HBcAg), DNA, polymerase","22 nm spheres and filaments: HBsAg only (non-infectious)","Genome: S, C, P, X genes; partially dsDNA"]],
["Serological markers",[["Marker","Significance"],["HBsAg","Infection; > 6 months = chronic"],["HBeAg","Active replication, high infectivity"],["IgM anti-HBc","Acute infection, window period"],["IgG anti-HBc","Past or chronic infection"],["Anti-HBe","Low replication"],["Anti-HBs","Immunity (recovery/vaccine)"],["HBV DNA","Viral load"]]],
["Prophylaxis",["Recombinant HBsAg vaccine: birth dose + 6, 10, 14 weeks (pentavalent)","HBIG + vaccine within 12 h for babies of HBsAg+ mothers","Post-exposure: HBIG + vaccine for non-immune","Blood donor screening, safe injection practices"]]],
{i:"About 300 million people worldwide live with chronic HBV; India has intermediate endemicity.",f:"Blood / sex / perinatal → hepatocyte (NTCP) → cccDNA\n → viral proteins → CTL attack → hepatitis\n ├ recovery (adults 95%)\n ├ fulminant\n └ chronic (neonates 90%) → cirrhosis → HCC",c:"HBV infection outcome depends on age at infection; serological markers define the phase, and universal infant vaccination with a birth dose is the cornerstone of prevention."});

Z("micro7",9,"m",["Hepatitis A is a faeco-orally spread RNA virus causing acute self-limiting hepatitis; hepatitis B is a blood/sexually spread DNA virus that can become chronic and cause cirrhosis and cancer.","HAV is a short-stay visitor through contaminated food and water; HBV is a long-term resident that can move in permanently.",["HAV: picornavirus, RNA, non-enveloped; incubation 2–6 weeks; no carrier state; children often mild.","HBV: hepadnavirus, DNA, enveloped; incubation 1–6 months; chronic carriers; cirrhosis, HCC.","HAV diagnosis: IgM anti-HAV; HBV: HBsAg, IgM anti-HBc.","Both have vaccines; HBV also has HBIG."],"HAV = 'Acute, A-oral, no chronic'; HBV = 'Blood, B-chronic, cancer'."],
"Hepatitis A virus is a picornavirus (Hepatovirus) — small (27 nm), non-enveloped, single-stranded positive-sense RNA, single serotype, stable to acid and heat; transmitted faeco-orally through contaminated food and water; incubation 15–45 days (average 4 weeks); children often have anicteric or mild disease, adults icteric hepatitis; peak infectivity before jaundice; no chronic carrier state, no cancer; fulminant hepatitis rare. Diagnosis: IgM anti-HAV (acute), IgG (immunity). Prevention: sanitation, safe water, inactivated or live attenuated vaccine, normal human immunoglobulin. Hepatitis B virus is a hepadnavirus — 42 nm, enveloped, partially double-stranded DNA with reverse transcriptase; transmitted parenterally, sexually and perinatally; incubation 30–180 days; insidious onset; carrier state and chronic hepatitis common (especially in infants), leading to cirrhosis and HCC; extrahepatic immune complex disease. Diagnosis: HBsAg, IgM anti-HBc, HBeAg, HBV DNA. Prevention: recombinant vaccine, HBIG.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Hepatitis A","Hepatitis B"],["Family","Picornavirus","Hepadnavirus"],["Genome","ssRNA (+)","Partially dsDNA"],["Envelope","No","Yes"],["Transmission","Faeco-oral","Parenteral, sexual, perinatal"],["Incubation","15–45 days","30–180 days"],["Onset","Acute","Insidious"],["Age","Children, young adults","Any age"],["Severity","Mild","More severe"],["Carrier state","No","Yes"],["Chronic hepatitis","No","Yes"],["Cirrhosis/HCC","No","Yes"],["Diagnosis","IgM anti-HAV","HBsAg, IgM anti-HBc"],["Vaccine","Inactivated/live","Recombinant HBsAg"],["Passive immunisation","Normal Ig","HBIG"]]]],
{c:"HAV and HBV differ in transmission, chronicity and long-term risk; both are vaccine-preventable."});

Z("micro7",10,"m",["HIV is a retrovirus that infects CD4 T cells and gradually destroys the immune system, leading to AIDS with opportunistic infections and cancers.","HIV attacks the 'generals' of the immune army (CD4 cells); without leaders, the army cannot defend against even weak invaders.",["Enveloped, two copies of + ssRNA, reverse transcriptase, integrase, protease; gp120 binds CD4, co-receptors CCR5/CXCR4; gp41 fusion.","Transmission: sexual (commonest), blood, mother-to-child (pregnancy, delivery, breastfeeding), needles.","Course: acute retroviral syndrome → clinical latency → symptomatic → AIDS (CD4 < 200 or AIDS-defining illness).","Lab: ELISA/rapid tests (3 different antigens/strategy in India — NACO), 4th-gen Ag/Ab, Western blot (old), p24 antigen, viral load (RT-PCR), CD4 count, DNA PCR for infants (< 18 months)."],"HIV stages: 'Acute → Asymptomatic → AIDS'; CD4 < 200 = AIDS."],
"Human immunodeficiency virus (Retroviridae, Lentivirus; HIV-1 worldwide, HIV-2 West Africa) is a spherical, enveloped virus (100–120 nm) with a cone-shaped capsid (p24) containing two copies of single-stranded positive-sense RNA, reverse transcriptase, integrase and protease; matrix protein p17; envelope glycoproteins gp120 (binds CD4) and gp41 (fusion). Genome: gag (core proteins), pol (enzymes), env (envelope) plus regulatory genes tat, rev, nef, vif, vpr, vpu. Replication: gp120 binds CD4 and chemokine co-receptor (CCR5 — macrophage-tropic; CXCR4 — T-tropic), gp41 mediates fusion; reverse transcription to dsDNA, integration into host DNA as provirus (latency), transcription, translation, protease cleavage and budding. Transmission: sexual intercourse (most common), blood and blood products, sharing needles, needle-stick injuries, mother-to-child (in utero, intrapartum, breastfeeding). Pathogenesis: infection of CD4 T cells, macrophages and dendritic cells; massive early depletion of gut CD4 cells; progressive CD4 T-cell loss by direct cytopathic effect, pyroptosis, CTL killing, apoptosis and chronic immune activation; viral reservoirs in latently infected memory cells; follicular dendritic cells trap virus in lymph nodes. Clinical course: acute HIV infection (2–6 weeks; mononucleosis-like illness, high viraemia), clinical latency (years; persistent generalised lymphadenopathy, continued viral replication), symptomatic phase (weight loss, fever, diarrhoea, oral candidiasis, herpes zoster), AIDS (CD4 < 200/µL or AIDS-defining conditions — opportunistic infections, Kaposi sarcoma, lymphomas, HIV encephalopathy, wasting). WHO clinical staging 1–4. Laboratory diagnosis: antibody detection — ELISA, rapid tests (NACO strategy: three tests with different antigens/principles for diagnosis), fourth-generation combined p24 antigen and antibody assays (reduced window period), Western blot/line immunoassay (confirmation in some settings); antigen — p24 ELISA; nucleic acid tests — HIV RNA viral load (RT-PCR; monitoring ART), proviral DNA PCR for infants < 18 months (maternal antibodies interfere); virus isolation; immunological — CD4 count (flow cytometry), CD4:CD8 ratio (reversed). Treatment: combination antiretroviral therapy (TLD — tenofovir, lamivudine, dolutegravir), 'test and treat'. Prevention: safe sex, condoms, PrEP, PEP, PPTCT, blood screening, harm reduction.",
[["Structure",["Envelope: gp120 (CD4 binding), gp41 (fusion)","Matrix p17; capsid p24","Two copies of ssRNA (+)","Enzymes: reverse transcriptase, integrase, protease","Genes: gag, pol, env, tat, rev, nef, vif, vpr, vpu"]],
["Clinical stages",[["Stage","Features"],["Acute infection","Fever, rash, lymphadenopathy, high viral load"],["Clinical latency","Asymptomatic, PGL, slow CD4 decline"],["Symptomatic","Weight loss, diarrhoea, oral thrush, zoster"],["AIDS","CD4 < 200, opportunistic infections, malignancies"]]],
["Laboratory diagnosis",[["Test","Use"],["ELISA / rapid tests","Screening, diagnosis (3-test strategy)"],["4th-generation Ag/Ab","Early detection"],["Western blot / LIA","Confirmation"],["p24 antigen","Window period"],["HIV RNA viral load","Monitoring ART"],["DNA PCR","Infants < 18 months"],["CD4 count","Staging, OI prophylaxis"]]]],
{i:"HIV/AIDS remains a major global infection; India has the third largest number of people living with HIV, though new infections have fallen with ART scale-up.",f:"gp120 + CD4 + CCR5/CXCR4 → fusion (gp41) → reverse transcription → integration (provirus)\n → replication → CD4 depletion + immune activation\n → acute → latency → AIDS (CD4 < 200) → opportunistic infections, tumours",c:"HIV destroys CD4 T cells, leading to AIDS; serological and molecular tests diagnose and monitor infection, and lifelong ART has transformed it into a manageable chronic disease."});

Z("micro7",11,"m",["Opportunistic infections are infections by organisms that rarely harm healthy people but cause serious disease in HIV patients when CD4 counts fall.","When the immune defences fall, 'opportunists' that were waiting quietly — TB, fungi, parasites, viruses — take over.",["Tuberculosis is the commonest OI in India at any CD4 count.","CD4 < 200: Pneumocystis pneumonia, oral/oesophageal candidiasis, toxoplasmosis (< 100), cryptococcal meningitis (< 100).","CD4 < 50: CMV retinitis, disseminated MAC, PML (JC virus), primary CNS lymphoma.","Prophylaxis: co-trimoxazole (PCP, toxo), INH preventive therapy (TB), fluconazole; ART restores immunity (watch for IRIS)."],"OIs by CD4: < 200 PCP, < 100 Toxo & Crypto, < 50 CMV & MAC."],
"Opportunistic infections (OIs) are infections caused by organisms that ordinarily do not cause disease in immunocompetent hosts but produce severe, recurrent or disseminated disease in immunocompromised individuals. In HIV infection their occurrence correlates with CD4 counts. Bacterial: tuberculosis (pulmonary and extrapulmonary; commonest OI in India and leading cause of death; occurs at any CD4), recurrent bacterial pneumonia, Salmonella septicaemia, Mycobacterium avium complex (disseminated, CD4 < 50). Fungal: oral and oesophageal candidiasis (CD4 < 200), Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (CD4 < 200; diffuse interstitial infiltrates, hypoxia; diagnosed by silver/Giemsa/IF stain of induced sputum or BAL), cryptococcal meningitis (CD4 < 100; India ink, CrAg), histoplasmosis, talaromycosis (penicilliosis, NE India), aspergillosis. Parasitic: cerebral toxoplasmosis (CD4 < 100; ring-enhancing lesions), cryptosporidiosis, cystoisosporiasis, cyclosporiasis, microsporidiosis (chronic diarrhoea), visceral leishmaniasis. Viral: CMV retinitis, colitis, encephalitis (CD4 < 50), herpes simplex (chronic ulcers), herpes zoster (multidermatomal), progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (JC virus), EBV (oral hairy leukoplakia, lymphomas), HHV-8 (Kaposi sarcoma), HPV (anogenital cancers). Diagnosis requires specific tests for each pathogen. Management: treat the OI, primary and secondary prophylaxis (co-trimoxazole for PCP and toxoplasmosis when CD4 < 200/350, isoniazid preventive therapy, fluconazole/CrAg screening, azithromycin for MAC), and start ART (with timing to minimise immune reconstitution inflammatory syndrome).",
[["OIs by CD4 count",[["CD4 (cells/µL)","Opportunistic infections"],["Any (< 500)","Tuberculosis, bacterial pneumonia, herpes zoster, oral candidiasis, Kaposi sarcoma"],["< 200","PCP, oesophageal candidiasis, cryptosporidiosis, PML, histoplasmosis"],["< 100","Cerebral toxoplasmosis, cryptococcal meningitis, talaromycosis"],["< 50","CMV retinitis, disseminated MAC, primary CNS lymphoma"]]],
["OIs by organism",[["Group","Examples"],["Bacteria","M. tuberculosis, MAC, Salmonella, pneumococcus"],["Fungi","Pneumocystis, Candida, Cryptococcus, Histoplasma, Talaromyces"],["Parasites","Toxoplasma, Cryptosporidium, Cystoisospora, Leishmania"],["Viruses","CMV, HSV, VZV, JC virus, EBV, HHV-8"]]],
["Prophylaxis",[["OI","Prophylaxis","When"],["PCP, toxoplasma","Co-trimoxazole","CD4 < 200 (India < 350)"],["TB","Isoniazid preventive therapy","All PLHIV after excluding active TB"],["Cryptococcus","CrAg screening, fluconazole","CD4 < 100"],["MAC","Azithromycin","CD4 < 50 (if not on ART)"]]]],
{i:"Opportunistic infections are the main cause of death in untreated HIV; their pattern follows the decline in CD4 count.",f:"HIV → progressive CD4 loss\n 500 → TB, zoster, candida\n 200 → PCP, oesophageal candida\n 100 → toxoplasma, cryptococcus\n 50 → CMV, MAC, PML, CNS lymphoma",c:"Knowing which OIs appear at which CD4 count guides diagnosis and prophylaxis; early ART prevents most opportunistic infections."});
